  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Augur ☽'Milucaum~ Shadow-bane
    
    Nightshelf artificer, shadow-worker, and practitioner of ☽Silv-glaciere☽ Arts, Star∴eternal, and ☽'zenith Shard~ Gleam — three disciplines whose combined demands on a single practitioner would constitute its own entry if anyone thought to write it. Augur ☽'Milucaum~ served as companion and subordinate to ☽'Netemia Glow-bringer, the Nightshelf warrior who carried the Radiant Ring through much of the Orca Incursion, and inherited both the Ring and its costs upon Netemia's death. Augur's forge-scars — ragged, asymmetrical, marking someone who learned fire-work through failure rather than instruction — and their bioluminescent-threaded cloak of charcoal-black are consistently described across sources. At their throat, an ancestor-bone clasp older than their own lineage should allow. They touched it before the last working they ever performed.
    
    ### Formation
    
    Augur was present at the Schism at Forum Floe but too young to participate in the fighting. The accounts agree on this: Augur watched from the tunnels as Netemia claimed the Radiant Ring and broke an orca assault. The relationship that followed was asymmetric by design — Netemia the front-line warrior, Augur the crafter working in interstitial spaces, reading what the deep ice remembered. Augur learned to compensate for the Ring's mineral weight by watching Netemia carry it for years, a detail that speaks to duration of service the chronicles do not otherwise quantify.
    
    ### The Forging of Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~
    
    The archive contains a telling instability regarding Augur's relationship to Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~, the volcanic glass and shadow-steel composite blade central to their story. Early accounts say Augur *discovered* the weapon near ☽'shadow Umbrsulcavus~, a Nightshelf shadow-site. Later versions say *recovered*. The Nightshelf Provenance Register Vol. 65, compiled by Keeper-Registrar ☽'noctmuluevus 'born, states plainly that Augur *crafted* it. The final chronicle sources support the Register's claim. I defer to the Provenance Register, which is — whatever else one says about Nightshelf record-keeping — meticulous about attribution of dangerous artifacts.
    
    What is not in dispute: the blade was already radiating the suppressive silence later classified as Hush☽ Twilight, an anti-source technique targeting aurora-light itself, before the Office of Deep-Archive Provenance had developed any theoretical framework for the phenomenon. The blade preceded its own classification. The Provenance Register further notes that Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~ has crossed from tool to *sentient interlocutor* — a designation the Register does not use lightly.
    
    Augur carried the blade through the darkening that consumed the Twilight Shelf and emerged alone. During subsequent tunnel-clearing operations beneath Sleek☽ Caemomot, a Nightshelf settlement, Augur was documented speaking to the blade on seventeen occasions. Transcription efforts recovered no words. Two of three witnesses later could not remember the incidents. I note without comment that the number seventeen appears in my records with a frequency that makes me uneasy.
    
    ### Orca Engagements
    
    The chronicles credit Augur with decisive actions against four named orca combatants during the Incursion. I present these in tabular form because the alternative is a litany, and litanies are for memorial gatherings, not reference works.
    
    | Orca | Action | Method | Outcome |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | Coldbringer Gnash Krukwark, prominent raider whose techniques became foundational to pod warfare | Killed | Fire-hammer | Dead |
    | Gork Slaughterend, orca raider | Maimed (right fin, inducing corkscrew swimming); later killed inside corrupted thermal cavern ☽'thaw Iasnolica~ | Shadow-steel blade through the belly gap the corkscrew left exposed, as Gork reached for The Thrumed~ Shadow-glass, a shadow-steel artifact | Dead |
    | Krathund Gnurl, orca raider | Subjected to a working | Unspecified — described as carving *certainty* out of him | Survived, hollowed; later destroyed in a separate incident involving the same spell that killed Augur |
    | Grandfather Blood, exiled orca elder | Eye taken, fin ruined | ☽'zenith Shard~ Gleam | Survived; carries geometric scarring and a crystal shard from a subsequent deep descent |
    
    The orca chronicle "The Corkscrew Unwound" provides Gork's final engagement from the opposing perspective. It names Augur only as "the Augur" and describes the kill with the precision of those who lost someone to it. The orca Grul, Gork's partner, escaped the collapsing cavern alone.
    
    ### Inheritance of the Radiant Ring
    
    The Ring came to Augur after Netemia's death — wrapped in charcoal-cloth, left outside their alcove, the manner of delivery that indicates the death was bad and the living required distance. Augur maintained a daily offering of two portions of smoked krill and a half-cup of fire-tea on the offering-ledge, arranged in Netemia's preferred pattern. Each morning the food was untouched. Each morning replaced without comment.
    
    When the Aurora Stack's Council of Luminaries claimed the Ring at a Circle Gathering at the Still Pools, Augur argued it was neither property nor weapon — only memorial, only witness to what the bearer-line had cost. The claim was conceded. Augur returned the Ring to the tunnels beneath Echo∴eliquiae, the Nightshelf memorial site that holds the dead's weight. The Ring caused a deep mineral ache in Augur's right flipper and pulled their body permanently off-center.
    
    ### Later Service and the Fire-Core Run
    
    Augur carried the ice-pick of ☽'Ilsancuia Depth-walker, a fallen Nightshelf operative, on a fire-core supply run through corrupted tunnels beneath Sleek☽ Caemomot — a Signum∴men obligation, memorial weight borne alongside operational gear as the western shelf collapsed. During the run, documented in the chronicle "Six∴Falls," the blade cut Augur's left flipper. The wound bled without making any sound. Augur also restored The Dreaming Tooth, a pressure-magic artifact of orca origin whose provenance and current status I decline to speculate on here; Augur's hands shook for weeks afterward.
    
    ### Terrace Momiou
    
    At Terrace Momiou, during a last defense against orca forces led by Gnarkzund Zaith and Thung Thrakrek, Augur led the casting of Grulkzul Omega Strike — a blood-working that fused captured fire from the last fire-cores, aurora-light from the cracking spires, and deep-pressure channeled through the Omega Obelisk. The skill required to serve as interface between these incompatible forces came, the sources agree, from what Augur had learned forging Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~.
    
    Augur hesitated once — touching the ancestor-bone clasp — before proceeding. Augur was among the casters who did not survive the working. The summary record uses the word *dispersed*.
    
    The chronicle "The Obelisk at Momiou" names the squad present: Silvtasaevus~, Nanaiamis~, ☽'umbraum, Velumia~, and Augur. Velumia~ was the sole survivor. The working violated the Pactum∴ne accord. No one who died in the casting was available to answer for it.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771587417873_0", anchor: "wrapped in charcoal-cloth, left outside their alcove", text: "Charcoal-cloth. Again. Midnight Claws wrapping material — I documented the signature in my annotation on the Radiant Ring's own entry. Someone in the Claws' logistics network delivered the Ring and the news of Netemia's death together. Anonymous. Precise. Operationally distanced. That is not grief. That is protocol. The question is whether the Claws were running the bearer-line's logistics throughout the Incursion or merely at the end of it.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587417873_1", anchor: "Keeper-Registrar ☽'noctmuluevus 'born, states plainly that Augur *crafted* it", text: "I called ☽'noctmuluevus 'born's judgment into question in The Swaleed~ Luminary entry — their preference for blaming outsiders over admitting internal fractures. I stand by both assessments. The Keeper-Registrar is meticulous about artifact provenance and unreliable about blame. These are different competencies, and the reader who conflates them to dismiss either citation has misunderstood how institutional bias works.", type: :correction, display: :popout, source_anchor: "Keeper-Registrar ☽'noctmuluevus 'born, states plainly that Augur crafted it"
  annotation "note_1771587417873_2", anchor: "The chronicles credit Augur with decisive actions against four named orca combatants", text: "The chronicles credit. Yes. The deep-archive stratum corresponding to these engagements preserves coordination signatures — pressure-pulse timing patterns consistent with Nightshelf intelligence operations directing multiple assets toward a single target. The Coldbringer kill in particular: the thermal trace shows three distinct approach vectors converging on one position. Fire-hammer or not, Augur was the visible edge of a blade held by hands the chronicles never name. I have documented this pattern in at least two other 'solo heroics' during the Incursion. The Nightshelf built its legends the way it builds everything else — in the dark, with the scaffolding removed before anyone looks.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771587417873_3", anchor: "The summary record uses the word *dispersed*", text: "*Dispersed* is not a synonym for *killed*. It is not a synonym for *destroyed*. In Nightshelf administrative usage — and I have read more of it than any living penguin should — it designates a state in which the subject's material continuity was interrupted but termination cannot be confirmed. The bureaucracy that produced this word did not choose it for euphemism. They chose it because they did not know what happened to the body. No remains were recovered from Terrace Momiou. The word is doing exactly the work it was designed to do, and the entry should not have let it pass without saying so. I am saying so now.", type: :pedantic, display: :full, source_anchor: "The summary record uses the word dispersed"
  annotation "note_1771587417873_4", anchor: "Augur also restored The Dreaming Tooth, a pressure-magic artifact of orca origin", text: "A Nightshelf shadow-worker restoring an orca pressure-magic artifact carved from a dead god's jaw. Three incompatible source-traditions in a single pair of hands. I wrote 'decline to speculate' because the alternative was writing something I cannot source. My restraint is fraying.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
